      ":question/id": "aaaaef0f-da20-591d-b9b1-480435d21a5b",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=5+t^{6}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=5+t^{6}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(5+t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(5+t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 5\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= 5\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+\\frac{t^{6+1}}{(6+1)}+C \\\\ &= 5t+\\frac{t^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
